          "id": "sec-4-5",
          "secNumber": "4.5",
          "heading": "Turbine performance and specific speed",
          "content": "## Lesson 4.3 \u2014 Turbine performance and specific speed\n\n**Physical basis.** Turbines extract mechanical energy from an upstream-to-downstream head difference. Hydraulic potential is rho*g*Q*H; realized mechanical power equals efficiency times this value. Unit selection depends on net head, discharge, speed and cavitation constraints.\n\n**Representative relation:** `P_out=eta*rho*g*Q*H`.\n\n**Assumptions and failure modes.** Net head excludes conveyance losses. Cross-machine performance comparisons should use dimensionless similarity parameters rather than nameplate power alone.\n\n**Investigation objective.** Estimate available hydraulic power at a small run-of-river site and list the missing data before turbine selection.\n\n### Why the model matters for an actual project\n\nThe decision in a micro-hydropower scheme depends on more than a single value of an unknown. Begin with an explicit sketch and a table listing the fluid state, channel or pipe geometry, the selected datum, boundary elevations and the intended output. Record the difference between a measured input and one chosen for an exploratory calculation. This distinction becomes essential when an optimistic coefficient produces a result that appears to satisfy a design target.\n\nFor turbine performance and specific speed, the equation `P_out=eta*rho*g*Q*H` can be used as a starting point, not a universal law independent of context. First trace every term back to the conservation principle or constitutive relation that supplies it. Next imagine reversing the direction of flow, doubling the characteristic dimension or moving the gauge datum. Some predictions must change in a specific direction; if the spreadsheet or simulation does not respect that monotonicity, stop and inspect the setup.\n\n### How to verify the result\n\nFor a flood-control lift station, collect a survey of cross-section geometry. A good verification uses at least one independently observable quantity, not the same calculation rearranged. Where a coefficient is empirical, report its source and the regime in which it was obtained. If the measured result differs from the model, examine datum errors, flow regime and equipment condition before altering a parameter merely to improve the fit.\n\n### Interpretation and limitations\n\nNet head excludes conveyance losses. Cross-machine performance comparisons should use dimensionless similarity parameters rather than nameplate power alone. In addition, surface roughness may vary with operating season or maintenance. A calculated response should therefore be accompanied by a bounded sensitivity study: change one influential variable, keep the others fixed, document whether the output rises or falls, and identify a physical observation that could falsify the assumed model. Engineers who cannot explain the direction of that change are not ready to use the output for selection.\n\n### Stepwise numerical practice \u2014 hydropower\n\nThe repeatable computation below uses **turbine output = eta rho g q h** and the relationship `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H`. Each row has a different operating situation. Students should first reproduce the central case by hand, then use code or a calculator to vary one input and check dimensional consistency. Because the data are synthetic, they are not evidence that any real plant or channel satisfies performance requirements.\n\n**Case 4.3.1: A high-altitude village intake.** Inputs: Flow = 0.83 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 18 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. The predicted **117.01 kW** comes from substituting these measurements into `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H` with consistent SI conversions. Because upstream water level can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain an independently calibrated pressure logger as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 4.3.2: A wastewater transfer station.** Inputs: Flow = 0.94 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 20 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. Using `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H`, the worked output is **147.25 kW**. A plausible error in surface roughness may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan a seasonal water-level record rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 4.3.3: A flood-control lift station.** Inputs: Flow = 1.05 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 22 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. The governing computation gives **180.93 kW**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how sediment accumulation affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a repeatable instrument zero check to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 4.3.4: A seasonal agricultural pump house.** Inputs: Flow = 1.16 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 24 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. The predicted **218.05 kW** comes from substituting these measurements into `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H` with consistent SI conversions. Because maintenance accessibility can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a controlled flow-stepping trial as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 4.3.5: A cooling-water circulation system.** Inputs: Flow = 1.27 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 26 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. Using `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H`, the worked output is **258.62 kW**. A plausible error in seasonal temperature change may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan an independently calibrated pressure logger rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 4.3.6: A reservoir booster station.** Inputs: Flow = 1.38 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 28 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. The governing computation gives **302.64 kW**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how instrument bias affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a seasonal water-level record to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 4.3.7: A canal-lift pumping station.** Inputs: Flow = 1.49 m\u00b3/s; Net head = 30 m; Efficiency = 0.8 \u2013. The predicted **350.1 kW** comes from substituting these measurements into `P=eta*rho*g*Q*H` with consistent SI conversions. Because boundary-condition uncertainty can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a repeatable instrument zero check as the external validation measurement.\n\n### Concept and transfer questions\n\n1. Starting with a control-volume or free-body diagram, explain why `P_out=eta*rho*g*Q*H` follows from the stated assumptions.\n2. In a cooling-water circulation system, identify two reasons a measured value could differ from this simplified estimate.\n3. Describe a feasible calibration or field test that would bound uncertainty in sediment accumulation.\n4. Give an example in which use of the relationship outside its validity range could reverse a project decision.\n\n**Mini-design exercise.** For a reservoir booster station, specify the physical layout, relevant upstream and downstream boundary data, the likely controlling mechanism, a conservative operating scenario, and the evidence that would be required before approving a change. Give an uncertainty range, not only a single final number."
