          "content": "## Lesson 6.2 \u2014 Venturi meters and differential pressure\n\n**Physical basis.** A Venturi meter infers discharge from continuity and an energy difference across a contraction, with a discharge coefficient accounting for nonideal behavior. It is particularly sensitive to pressure-sensor calibration and density assumptions.\n\n**Representative relation:** `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))`.\n\n**Assumptions and failure modes.** Place taps and lengths according to the instrument design. Two-phase flow, severe swirl or clogged pressure lines invalidate the simple Bernoulli-based result.\n\n**Investigation objective.** Compare predicted Venturi discharge with a timed volumetric collection and calculate calibration bias.\n\n### Why the model matters for an actual project\n\nThe decision in a factory-process water Venturi depends on more than a single value of an unknown. Begin with an explicit sketch and a table listing the fluid state, channel or pipe geometry, the selected datum, boundary elevations and the intended output. Record the difference between a measured input and one chosen for an exploratory calculation. This distinction becomes essential when an optimistic coefficient produces a result that appears to satisfy a design target.\n\nFor venturi meters and differential pressure, the equation `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))` can be used as a starting point, not a universal law independent of context. First trace every term back to the conservation principle or constitutive relation that supplies it. Next imagine reversing the direction of flow, doubling the characteristic dimension or moving the gauge datum. Some predictions must change in a specific direction; if the spreadsheet or simulation does not respect that monotonicity, stop and inspect the setup.\n\n### How to verify the result\n\nFor a stormwater reporting station, collect a controlled flow-stepping trial. A good verification uses at least one independently observable quantity, not the same calculation rearranged. Where a coefficient is empirical, report its source and the regime in which it was obtained. If the measured result differs from the model, examine datum errors, flow regime and equipment condition before altering a parameter merely to improve the fit.\n\n### Interpretation and limitations\n\nPlace taps and lengths according to the instrument design. Two-phase flow, severe swirl or clogged pressure lines invalidate the simple Bernoulli-based result. In addition, upstream water level may vary with operating season or maintenance. A calculated response should therefore be accompanied by a bounded sensitivity study: change one influential variable, keep the others fixed, document whether the output rises or falls, and identify a physical observation that could falsify the assumed model. Engineers who cannot explain the direction of that change are not ready to use the output for selection.\n\n### Stepwise numerical practice \u2014 Venturi discharge\n\nThe repeatable computation below uses **venturi flow from differential pressure and contraction** and the relationship `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))`. Each row has a different operating situation. Students should first reproduce the central case by hand, then use code or a calculator to vary one input and check dimensional consistency. Because the data are synthetic, they are not evidence that any real plant or channel satisfies performance requirements.\n\n**Case 6.2.1: A stream-gauging station.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 12 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. The predicted **47.427 L/s** comes from substituting these measurements into `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))` with consistent SI conversions. Because operating schedule can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a survey of cross-section geometry as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 6.2.2: An experimental flume throat.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 14 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. Using `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))`, the worked output is **51.227 L/s**. A plausible error in upstream water level may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan a timed volumetric collection measurement rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 6.2.3: A stormwater reporting station.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 16 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. The governing computation gives **54.764 L/s**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how surface roughness affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a field roughness estimate to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 6.2.4: A groundwater recharge basin outlet.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 18 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. The predicted **58.086 L/s** comes from substituting these measurements into `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))` with consistent SI conversions. Because sediment accumulation can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a downstream tailwater survey as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 6.2.5: A natural-channel rating site.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 20 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. Using `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))`, the worked output is **61.228 L/s**. A plausible error in maintenance accessibility may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan a survey of cross-section geometry rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 6.2.6: A flood-monitoring pilot station.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 22 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. The governing computation gives **64.217 L/s**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how seasonal temperature change affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a timed volumetric collection measurement to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 6.2.7: A rural irrigation weir.** Inputs: Pressure difference = 24 kPa; Inlet diameter = 0.2 m; Throat diameter = 0.11 m; Coefficient = 0.97 \u2013. The predicted **67.072 L/s** comes from substituting these measurements into `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))` with consistent SI conversions. Because instrument bias can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a field roughness estimate as the external validation measurement.\n\n### Concept and transfer questions\n\n1. Starting with a control-volume or free-body diagram, explain why `Q=Cd*A2*sqrt((2*dp/rho)/(1-beta^4))` follows from the stated assumptions.\n2. In a natural-channel rating site, identify two reasons a measured value could differ from this simplified estimate.\n3. Describe a feasible calibration or field test that would bound uncertainty in surface roughness.\n4. Give an example in which use of the relationship outside its validity range could reverse a project decision.\n\n**Mini-design exercise.** For a flood-monitoring pilot station, specify the physical layout, relevant upstream and downstream boundary data, the likely controlling mechanism, a conservative operating scenario, and the evidence that would be required before approving a change. Give an uncertainty range, not only a single final number."
