          "id": "sec-6-5",
          "secNumber": "6.5",
          "heading": "Flumes and rating curves",
          "content": "## Lesson 6.3 \u2014 Flumes and rating curves\n\n**Physical basis.** Flumes create a hydraulic control in a channel that connects a measured upstream depth with discharge. A rating curve represents geometry and flow conditions; a changed bed, downstream submergence or deposited sediment can change the relation.\n\n**Representative relation:** `Q=C*(h-h0)^m (empirical calibration)`.\n\n**Assumptions and failure modes.** Do not extrapolate a fitted rating curve far outside the calibration range. Record whether the flow is modular/free or submerged.\n\n**Investigation objective.** Fit a power-law rating to observations and explain what residual pattern indicates a poor model.\n\n### Why the model matters for an actual project\n\nThe decision in a stream-gauging station depends on more than a single value of an unknown. Begin with an explicit sketch and a table listing the fluid state, channel or pipe geometry, the selected datum, boundary elevations and the intended output. Record the difference between a measured input and one chosen for an exploratory calculation. This distinction becomes essential when an optimistic coefficient produces a result that appears to satisfy a design target.\n\nFor flumes and rating curves, the equation `Q=C*(h-h0)^m (empirical calibration)` can be used as a starting point, not a universal law independent of context. First trace every term back to the conservation principle or constitutive relation that supplies it. Next imagine reversing the direction of flow, doubling the characteristic dimension or moving the gauge datum. Some predictions must change in a specific direction; if the spreadsheet or simulation does not respect that monotonicity, stop and inspect the setup.\n\n### How to verify the result\n\nFor a groundwater recharge basin outlet, collect a survey of cross-section geometry. A good verification uses at least one independently observable quantity, not the same calculation rearranged. Where a coefficient is empirical, report its source and the regime in which it was obtained. If the measured result differs from the model, examine datum errors, flow regime and equipment condition before altering a parameter merely to improve the fit.\n\n### Interpretation and limitations\n\nDo not extrapolate a fitted rating curve far outside the calibration range. Record whether the flow is modular/free or submerged. In addition, surface roughness may vary with operating season or maintenance. A calculated response should therefore be accompanied by a bounded sensitivity study: change one influential variable, keep the others fixed, document whether the output rises or falls, and identify a physical observation that could falsify the assumed model. Engineers who cannot explain the direction of that change are not ready to use the output for selection.\n\n### Stepwise numerical practice \u2014 rating-curve estimate\n\nThe repeatable computation below uses **a calibrated power-law relation q=c(h-h0)^m** and the relationship `Q=C*(h-h0)^m`. Each row has a different operating situation. Students should first reproduce the central case by hand, then use code or a calculator to vary one input and check dimensional consistency. Because the data are synthetic, they are not evidence that any real plant or channel satisfies performance requirements.\n\n**Case 6.3.1: An experimental flume throat.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.57 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. The predicted **0.69739 m\u00b3/s** comes from substituting these measurements into `Q=C*(h-h0)^m` with consistent SI conversions. Because upstream water level can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain an independently calibrated pressure logger as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 6.3.2: A stormwater reporting station.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.61 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. Using `Q=C*(h-h0)^m`, the worked output is **0.77651 m\u00b3/s**. A plausible error in surface roughness may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan a seasonal water-level record rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 6.3.3: A groundwater recharge basin outlet.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.65 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. The governing computation gives **0.85821 m\u00b3/s**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `Q=C*(h-h0)^m`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how sediment accumulation affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a repeatable instrument zero check to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 6.3.4: A natural-channel rating site.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.69 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. The predicted **0.9424 m\u00b3/s** comes from substituting these measurements into `Q=C*(h-h0)^m` with consistent SI conversions. Because maintenance accessibility can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a controlled flow-stepping trial as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 6.3.5: A flood-monitoring pilot station.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.73 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. Using `Q=C*(h-h0)^m`, the worked output is **1.029 m\u00b3/s**. A plausible error in seasonal temperature change may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan an independently calibrated pressure logger rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 6.3.6: A rural irrigation weir.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.77 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. The governing computation gives **1.1179 m\u00b3/s**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `Q=C*(h-h0)^m`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how instrument bias affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a seasonal water-level record to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 6.3.7: A factory-process water venturi.** Inputs: Gauge head = 0.81 m; Rating coefficient = 1.8 context dependent; Exponent = 1.45 \u2013; Offset = 0.05 m. The predicted **1.2091 m\u00b3/s** comes from substituting these measurements into `Q=C*(h-h0)^m` with consistent SI conversions. Because boundary-condition uncertainty can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a repeatable instrument zero check as the external validation measurement.\n\n### Concept and transfer questions\n\n1. Starting with a control-volume or free-body diagram, explain why `Q=C*(h-h0)^m (empirical calibration)` follows from the stated assumptions.\n2. In a flood-monitoring pilot station, identify two reasons a measured value could differ from this simplified estimate.\n3. Describe a feasible calibration or field test that would bound uncertainty in sediment accumulation.\n4. Give an example in which use of the relationship outside its validity range could reverse a project decision.\n\n**Mini-design exercise.** For a rural irrigation weir, specify the physical layout, relevant upstream and downstream boundary data, the likely controlling mechanism, a conservative operating scenario, and the evidence that would be required before approving a change. Give an uncertainty range, not only a single final number."
