          "heading": "Uncertainty and calibration",
          "content": "## Lesson 6.4 \u2014 Uncertainty and calibration\n\n**Physical basis.** Measurement uncertainty includes repeatability, bias, resolution, calibration reference and physical model error. Propagate uncertainty through nonlinear relations using derivatives or Monte Carlo experiments, then distinguish numerical precision from defensible accuracy.\n\n**Representative relation:** `u_Q\u2248sqrt(\u03a3((\u2202Q/\u2202x_i)*u_i)^2)`.\n\n**Assumptions and failure modes.** A smooth plot and many decimal places cannot rescue biased head readings or an incorrect coefficient. Report uncertainty assumptions and traceability alongside flow estimates.\n\n**Investigation objective.** Compare a flowmeter before and after zero adjustment and design a calibration verification schedule.\n\n### Why the model matters for an actual project\n\nThe decision in an experimental flume throat depends on more than a single value of an unknown. Begin with an explicit sketch and a table listing the fluid state, channel or pipe geometry, the selected datum, boundary elevations and the intended output. Record the difference between a measured input and one chosen for an exploratory calculation. This distinction becomes essential when an optimistic coefficient produces a result that appears to satisfy a design target.\n\nFor uncertainty and calibration, the equation `u_Q\u2248sqrt(\u03a3((\u2202Q/\u2202x_i)*u_i)^2)` can be used as a starting point, not a universal law independent of context. First trace every term back to the conservation principle or constitutive relation that supplies it. Next imagine reversing the direction of flow, doubling the characteristic dimension or moving the gauge datum. Some predictions must change in a specific direction; if the spreadsheet or simulation does not respect that monotonicity, stop and inspect the setup.\n\n### How to verify the result\n\nFor a natural-channel rating site, collect an independently calibrated pressure logger. A good verification uses at least one independently observable quantity, not the same calculation rearranged. Where a coefficient is empirical, report its source and the regime in which it was obtained. If the measured result differs from the model, examine datum errors, flow regime and equipment condition before altering a parameter merely to improve the fit.\n\n### Interpretation and limitations\n\nA smooth plot and many decimal places cannot rescue biased head readings or an incorrect coefficient. Report uncertainty assumptions and traceability alongside flow estimates. In addition, sediment accumulation may vary with operating season or maintenance. A calculated response should therefore be accompanied by a bounded sensitivity study: change one influential variable, keep the others fixed, document whether the output rises or falls, and identify a physical observation that could falsify the assumed model. Engineers who cannot explain the direction of that change are not ready to use the output for selection.\n\n### Stepwise numerical practice \u2014 head measurement sensitivity\n\nThe repeatable computation below uses **weir q scales approximately with h^1.5** and the relationship `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5`. Each row has a different operating situation. Students should first reproduce the central case by hand, then use code or a calculator to vary one input and check dimensional consistency. Because the data are synthetic, they are not evidence that any real plant or channel satisfies performance requirements.\n\n**Case 6.4.1: A stormwater reporting station.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.33 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. The predicted **1.0228 Q ratio** comes from substituting these measurements into `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5` with consistent SI conversions. Because surface roughness can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a timed volumetric collection measurement as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 6.4.2: A groundwater recharge basin outlet.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.35 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. Using `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5`, the worked output is **1.0215 Q ratio**. A plausible error in sediment accumulation may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan a field roughness estimate rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 6.4.3: A natural-channel rating site.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.37 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. The governing computation gives **1.0203 Q ratio**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how maintenance accessibility affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a downstream tailwater survey to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 6.4.4: A flood-monitoring pilot station.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.39 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. The predicted **1.0193 Q ratio** comes from substituting these measurements into `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5` with consistent SI conversions. Because seasonal temperature change can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a survey of cross-section geometry as the external validation measurement.\n\n**Case 6.4.5: A rural irrigation weir.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.41 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. Using `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5`, the worked output is **1.0183 Q ratio**. A plausible error in instrument bias may dominate the last displayed digit. Show each intermediate step, then state whether a larger first listed parameter would move the result in the expected physical direction. Plan a timed volumetric collection measurement rather than calibrating the equation only against its own output.\n\n**Case 6.4.6: A factory-process water venturi.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.43 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. The governing computation gives **1.0175 Q ratio**. Sketch the geometry and trace how each input enters `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5`. To avoid false precision, perturb the least certain input, discuss how boundary-condition uncertainty affects the chosen boundary condition, and recommend a field roughness estimate to challenge the prediction independently.\n\n**Case 6.4.7: A stream-gauging station.** Inputs: Reference head = 0.45 m; Head offset = 0.005 m. The predicted **1.0167 Q ratio** comes from substituting these measurements into `Qratio=(H2/H1)^1.5` with consistent SI conversions. Because operating schedule can shift in service, evaluate a second condition before interpreting the point estimate as a robust capacity. Obtain a downstream tailwater survey as the external validation measurement.\n\n### Concept and transfer questions\n\n1. Starting with a control-volume or free-body diagram, explain why `u_Q\u2248sqrt(\u03a3((\u2202Q/\u2202x_i)*u_i)^2)` follows from the stated assumptions.\n2. In a rural irrigation weir, identify two reasons a measured value could differ from this simplified estimate.\n3. Describe a feasible calibration or field test that would bound uncertainty in maintenance accessibility.\n4. Give an example in which use of the relationship outside its validity range could reverse a project decision.\n\n**Mini-design exercise.** For a factory-process water Venturi, specify the physical layout, relevant upstream and downstream boundary data, the likely controlling mechanism, a conservative operating scenario, and the evidence that would be required before approving a change. Give an uncertainty range, not only a single final number."
